It's *possible* for the Wizard to steal a quest artifact.  That doesn't
mean it's *likely*.

Let's have a quick look at the player's equipment slots:
Hand 1
Hand 2
Ring
Amulet
Armor
Cloak
Gloves
Boots
Helm
Shirt

The most versitile of these is Ring, since the player can get two of
them and there's a wide variety of equipment that can go there.
Because of this, most Rings are relatively low-powered.

Among the least versitile are Cloak, Amulet and Armor: there are only a
few special things that can go into each of these slots, and the player
can only have one of them anyway.  Some of these things are things a
player can get nowhere else: Cloak is the only way a player can get
displacement.  Amulet is the only way to get Life Saving.

It would be nice for these cool abilities to be useful in the late
game, but all too often they are ignored because the player has an
ascension checklist to meet.  In vanilla Nethack, one of the biggies is
magic resistance, which would be very desirable even if it wasn't the
only reasonable way to escape certain unheralded instadeaths.

If you want to be absolutely sure you aren't vulnerable to death in two
turns ("So thou thoughtst thou could kill me, fool...", "Oh no!  He's
using the touch of death!", "Do you want your possessions identified?")
most any time after you've woken up the Wizard, then you need magic
resistance.  And those people who notice that Rodney can swipe a quest
artifact source of MR and use touch of death in the single turn do have
a point: if you want to be absolutely safe then you need a non-artifact
source of MR, and there's only two of those.

I observe that a game in which there are many different viable
strategies for winning is richer than a game in which players must
always collect what amounts to the same things.  As it is, anyone who
isn't specifically trying a challenge ascension like Nudist, which is
supposed to be hard anyway, must have one of those two pieces of
equipment.  While I like the aspects of your patch that make resistance
rings more useful, I'm less happy with *further* restricting the
methods available of avoiding sudden game-ending due to touch of death.


